{
  "dom_id": "step-q172314",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "$\\,$ $|2n-8|=4$ \n\nWhich of the following conditions are sufficient for the above to be true?\n\nI. $|n|<7$\n\nII. $n<0$\n\nIII. $n=6$",
  "worked_solution": "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *sufficient* for the given one:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,(|2n-8|=4)\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2n-8| & =4 \\\\ 2n-8 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ n & =2,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(|n|<7)\\,⇏\\,(|2n-8|=4)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $n=3,$ then  $|n|<7$, but\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2n-8| & =|2(3)-8| \\\\  & =|-2| \\\\  & \\ne 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition II is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(n<0)\\,⇏\\,(|2n-8|=4)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $n=-1,$ then  $n<0$, but\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2n-8| & =|2(-1)-8| \\\\  & =|-10| \\\\  & \\ne 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition III is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(n=6)\\,⇒\\,(|2n-8|=4)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $n=6,$ then $|2n-8|=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
